# SHA-256 of every deterministic output of the pinned run.
#
# Pinned run: 183 complete HSV-1 genomes, retrieved 2026-09-07T21:56:25Z.
# Manifest generated 2026-09-08T16:13:40Z
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Most of results/ is not version-controlled: it is ~4 MB of
# regenerable per-guide and per-site enumeration, and carrying it would make the
# repository worse, not better. But "regenerable" is a claim, and a claim a reviewer
# cannot check is not evidence. These hashes let anyone confirm that a regenerated
# copy is identical to the one the manuscript was written from, without the
# repository having to ship the file.
#
# LINE ENDINGS ARE NORMALISED BEFORE HASHING. Each hash is over the file content with
# every CRLF (and every lone CR) replaced by LF. The pipeline writes CRLF on Windows
# and LF elsewhere, and git itself rewrites line endings on checkout, so hashing the
# raw bytes would make this manifest platform-specific and it would spuriously fail
# for a reviewer on Linux or macOS. This means `sha256sum -c` will NOT agree with this
# file on a machine whose checkout uses CRLF; use verify.py, which normalises the same
# way, or on an LF platform:
#     import hashlib, pathlib, sys
#     b = pathlib.Path(sys.argv[1]).read_bytes()
#     b = b.replace(b'\r\n', b'\n').replace(b'\r', b'\n')
#     print(hashlib.sha256(b).hexdigest())
#
# HOW TO CHECK.
#     python verify.py --rerun          # recomputes stages 2-4 and checks these hashes
#     python verify.py --rerun --full   # also stages 6 and 7
#
# WHAT IS NOT LISTED, and why. This manifest cannot hash itself. Twelve further files
# embed a generation timestamp, the invoking command line, or a measured wall-clock
# duration, so they can never hash equal across runs even when every scientific value
# in them is identical:
#
#     results/escape_model_report.md
#     results/escape_summary.json
#     results/offtarget_report.md
#     results/offtarget_summary.json
#     results/robustness_report.md
#     results/robustness_summary.json
#     results/run_log.json
#     results/sacas9/run_log.json
#     results/sacas9-20nt/run_log.json
#     results/sacas9-nngrrn/run_log.json
#     results/sacas9_benchmark_report.md
#     results/sacas9_benchmark_summary.json
#
# The nine top-level files in that list are themselves version-controlled, so they
# can be diffed directly; the three under results/sacas9*/ are alternative-nuclease
# re-runs of stages 2-4 and are regenerated on demand. A re-run should differ from any
# of them ONLY in timestamps, the recorded command line and measured durations; any
# other difference is a real change and should be treated as one.
#
# Format is the usual "<sha256>  <path>", paths relative to the repository root and
# with forward slashes on every platform.

